---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PosterBand from "../components/PosterBand.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import RecordFigure from "../components/RecordFigure.astro";
import CloseStrip from "../components/CloseStrip.astro";
import treasuryHoldings from "../assets/evidence/treasury-holdings-2025-06-30.png";
import apersHoldings from "../assets/evidence/apers-holdings-2026-09-07.png";
import atrsBond from "../assets/evidence/atrs-israel-bond-2026-02-15.png";

const contents = [
  ["#witness", "Marwa’s story"],
  ["#responsibility", "Israel’s assault on Gaza"],
  ["#palestinian-call", "The Palestinian call"],
  ["#arkansas", "Arkansas’s choices"],
  ["#treasury", "State Treasury"],
  ["#public-employees", "Public employees"],
  ["#teachers", "Teachers’ retirement"],
];
---

<Base path="/the-issue/">
  <PosterBand path="/the-issue/">
    <Fragment slot="title">Palestinian life.<br /><em>Palestinian freedom.</em></Fragment>
    <p slot="intro">
      A future in which people can learn, heal, raise their families, and live freely. That is what our solidarity is for.
    </p>
    <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="onpage__label">On this page</p>
      <ol>{contents.map(([href, label]) => <li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>
  </PosterBand>

  <div class="wrap issue">
    <section class="margin-grid chapter" id="witness" aria-labelledby="witness-title">
      <h2 id="witness-title">To study. To heal.<br /><em>To live.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">Marwa · Gaza · March 2024</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Marwa was in her fourth year at Al-Azhar University in Gaza. She and her sister tried to find a little escape in
          novels. Her brothers fetched water; her father bought food. Her diary describes the daily work of keeping a family
          going.
        </p>
        <p>
          She recalled being forced to leave a shelter in Khan Younis and reaching Rafah on her fourth displacement. The family
          eventually built a tent on the beach, exposed to wind and cold.
        </p>
        <p>
          She wanted to finish her education, bring her family to safety, and rebuild their life. Those hopes run through her
          account alongside fear, grief, and anger.
        </p>
        <a class="go" href="https://www.unicef.org/stories/day-gaza-marwas-diary"
          >Read and listen to her diary at UNICEF <Icon name="external" /></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid chapter" id="responsibility" aria-labelledby="responsibility-title">
      <h2 id="responsibility-title">Israel is destroying<br /><em>the conditions of life.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">Israel’s assault on Gaza</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The Israeli government and military have destroyed Palestinian homes, attacked hospitals, and forcibly displaced
          people in Gaza. Al-Haq’s January 2025 report documents the systematic assault on healthcare, including attacks on
          medical facilities and the denial of lifesaving care, as a pattern of genocide.
        </p>
        <p>
          In July 2025, the Israeli human rights organization B’Tselem concluded that Israel was committing genocide against
          Palestinians in Gaza. Its report describes killing, displacement, destruction of civilian life, and the policies
          enabling them.
        </p>
        <p class="statement">Arkansas must end its financial support for Israel’s genocide and apartheid.</p>
        <div class="stack">
          <a class="go" href="https://www.alhaq.org/publications/25846.html"
            >Read Al-Haq’s healthcare report <Icon name="external" /></a
          >
          <a class="go" href="https://www.btselem.org/sites/default/files/publications/202507_our_genocide_eng.pdf"
            >Read B’Tselem’s Our Genocide (PDF) <Icon name="external" /></a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid chapter" id="palestinian-call" aria-labelledby="call-title">
      <h2 id="call-title">Freedom. Equality.<br /><em>Return.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">The Palestinian call · July 2005</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Palestinian unions, women’s organizations, refugee groups, and other civil society organizations called on people
          around the world to:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="call-quote">
          <p>“impose broad boycotts and implement divestment initiatives against Israel”</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>Their call for boycott, divestment, and sanctions sets out three demands:</p>
        <ul class="demands">
          <li>End Israel’s occupation and colonization of Arab lands and dismantle the Wall.</li>
          <li>Ensure full equality for Palestinian citizens of Israel.</li>
          <li>Uphold Palestinian refugees’ right to return to their homes and properties.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>We can answer that call here by organizing to end Arkansas’s financial support for the Israeli state.</p>
        <a class="go" href="https://bdsmovement.net/call"
          >Read the full call and its Palestinian signatories <Icon name="external" /></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid chapter" id="arkansas" aria-labelledby="arkansas-title">
      <h2 id="arkansas-title">Public money.<br /><em>Direct state support.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">The Arkansas connection</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Buying an Israel Bond means lending money to the State of Israel. The issuer says the government can use the
          proceeds for purposes it determines.
        </p>
        <p>
          Arkansas’s Treasury and retirement systems have made choices to hold, purchase, or fund Israel Bonds investments.
          The records below establish those choices. They give our demand a local target.
        </p>
        <details class="disclosure">
          <summary>How Israel Bonds finance the state <Icon name="plus" /></summary>
          <div class="panel">
            <p>
              Israel Bonds describes these securities as direct obligations of the State of Israel, with proceeds available for
              government-determined purposes. This establishes state financing; it does not trace a particular Arkansas dollar
              to a particular military attack.
            </p>
            <a class="go" href="https://israelbonds.com/knowledge-base/about-israel-bonds/"
              >Read the issuer’s explanation <Icon name="external" /></a
            >
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid institution" id="treasury" aria-labelledby="treasury-title">
      <h2 id="treasury-title">$55 million<br /><em>held in Israel Bonds.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">Arkansas State Treasury</p>
      <p class="provenance">Bondholder statement · June 30, 2025</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The Treasury’s statement lists seven Israel Bonds positions totaling $55 million, with individual maturity dates.
          Among them: $10 million scheduled to mature in October 2026 and another $10 million in November 2026.
        </p>
        <p>The demand is to stop new purchases and prevent money returning to Israel Bonds when existing investments mature.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wide">
        <RecordFigure
          image={treasuryHoldings}
          href="/media/treasury-holdings-2025-06-30.png"
          label="Source record"
          alt="Scanned table headed “State of Israel – Bondholder Statement 30 Jun 2025”, account information excluding transactions pending settlement. Seven Israel Bonds are listed with interest rate, issue date, amount, maturity date and CUSIP; maturities run from 01 Feb 2026 to 01 May 2030, including two $5,000,000 bonds due 01 Oct 2026 and a $10,000,000 bond due 01 Nov 2026."
        >
          State of Israel bondholder statement for the Arkansas Treasury, June 30, 2025. Produced in the Treasury’s February 19,
          2026 public-records response. A literal crop of the dated holdings table. Select the image to open it at full size. <a
            href="/evidence/#SRC-TRE-001">Source notes</a
          >.
        </RecordFigure>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <details class="disclosure">
          <summary>See the holdings and maturity record <Icon name="plus" /></summary>
          <div class="panel">
            <p>
              State of Israel bondholder statement, June 30, 2025. Produced in the Treasury’s February 19, 2026 public-records
              response, PDF page 1. The linked image is a literal crop of the dated holdings table.
            </p>
            <a class="go" href="/media/treasury-holdings-2025-06-30.png">Open the dated holdings table <Icon name="external" /></a>
            <p class="provenance">
              The statement is historical. A later Treasury inventory adjustment records a $5 million February 2026 maturity.
              The $55 million figure above is not presented as today’s balance.
            </p>
            <p>
              <a href="/evidence/#SRC-TRE-001">Read the fuller source and publication notes</a> · <a href="/investments/"
                >View the dated ledger</a
              >
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid institution" id="public-employees" aria-labelledby="apers-title">
      <h2 id="apers-title">$25 million<br /><em>held.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">Arkansas Public Employees Retirement System</p>
      <p class="provenance">Preliminary custody report · September 7, 2026</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          APERS used retirement-system money to buy $15 million in Israel Bonds on October 15, 2025. A custody report APERS
          produced, dated September 7, 2026 and marked preliminary, lists that bond and a second $10 million Israel Bond: $25
          million on that report.
        </p>
        <p>
          Public employees and retirees have a reason to demand that their retirement system stop financing the Israeli
          government.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="wide">
        <RecordFigure
          image={apersHoldings}
          href="/media/apers-holdings-2026-09-07.png"
          label="Source record"
          alt="BNY “Asset and Accrual Detail – By Asset type” report dated 09/07/2026, status preliminary, with most rows blacked out. Under “Israel”: 10,000,000 of 46515DRC8, 4.670% maturing 08/01/2028, and 15,000,000 of 46514X2A6, 4.060% maturing 10/01/2027. Subtotal Israel: 25,000,000.00."
        >
          BNY custody report for APERS dated September 7, 2026 and marked preliminary. Produced in APERS’s September 9, 2026
          public-records response. A literal crop; the black boxes are the agency’s redactions. Select the image to open it at
          full size. <a href="/evidence/#SRC-APE-002">Source notes</a>.
        </RecordFigure>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <details class="disclosure">
          <summary>See the holdings and purchase records <Icon name="plus" /></summary>
          <div class="panel">
            <p>
              APERS public-records response, September 9, 2026: a BNY Asset and Accrual Detail report dated September 7, 2026
              and marked preliminary, page 1. The linked image is a literal crop of its heading and Israel Bonds rows.
            </p>
            <a class="go" href="/media/apers-holdings-2026-09-07.png"
              >Open the $25 million holdings excerpt <Icon name="external" /></a
            >
            <p>
              APERS public-records response, February 27, 2026, PDF pages 6925 and 6929. These literal source crops show the
              transfer amount and the email identifying the initial purchase date.
            </p>
            <div class="stack">
              <a class="go" href="/media/apers-purchase-amount.png"
                >Open the $15 million transaction excerpt <Icon name="external" /></a
              >
              <a class="go" href="/media/apers-purchase-date.png">Open the purchase-date email excerpt <Icon name="external" /></a>
            </div>
            <p>
              <a href="/evidence/#SRC-APE-002">Holdings report notes</a> · <a href="/evidence/#SRC-APE-001"
                >Purchase record notes</a
              > · <a href="/investments/#apers">View the dated ledger</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid institution" id="teachers" aria-labelledby="atrs-title">
      <h2 id="atrs-title">$50 million funded.<br /><em>$9.9 million bought.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">Arkansas Teacher Retirement System</p>
      <p class="provenance">Funding · January 2, 2026 · Purchase · February 2026</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          On January 2, 2026, ATRS moved $50 million into the Scout/Reams account set up for its Israel Bonds mandate. In
          February the manager bought a $9.9 million Israel Bond, issued February 15. The account report for July 31, 2026
          shows that bond still held, with most of the remaining money in U.S. Treasury notes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The manager’s second-quarter report states a long-term objective of a laddered Israel Bonds portfolio, buying no
          more than $10 million in a calendar year, and says it bought a ladder of U.S. Treasury notes so their maturities
          would line up with those future purchases. The earliest matures January 15, 2027.
        </p>
        <p>
          Teachers and retirees can demand that ATRS stop the next purchase and end this mandate’s support for the Israeli
          government.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="wide steps">
        <h3 class="steps__title">How the ATRS money moved</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <span class="steps__date">June 2, 2025</span>
            <span class="steps__state">Authorized</span>
            <span class="steps__amt">Up to $50 million</span>
            <span class="steps__note">Board approval for Israel Bonds managed through Scout/Reams</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="steps__date">January 2, 2026</span>
            <span class="steps__state">Funded</span>
            <span class="steps__amt">$50 million</span>
            <span class="steps__note">Moved into the Reams account</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="steps__date">February 2026</span>
            <span class="steps__state">Bought</span>
            <span class="steps__amt">$9.9 million</span>
            <span class="steps__note">One Israel Bond, issued February 15</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="steps__date">July 31, 2026</span>
            <span class="steps__state">Held</span>
            <span class="steps__amt">$9.9 million</span>
            <span class="steps__note"
              >19.71% of the account’s $50.4 million market value; the rest was U.S. Treasury notes and cash</span
            >
            <span class="steps__bar" aria-hidden="true"><span></span></span>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="steps__foot">
          The manager’s second-quarter report states a long-term objective of a laddered Israel Bonds portfolio, buying no more
          than $10 million in a calendar year. <a href="/investments/#atrs">See the ATRS records</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="wide">
        <RecordFigure
          image={atrsBond}
          href="/media/atrs-israel-bond-2026-02-15.png"
          label="Source record"
          alt="Israel Bonds purchase confirmation addressed to the Arkansas Teachers Retirement System: “An Israel bond has been purchased in your name.” Issue 13th Institutional Jubilee Fix 5Y 4.93 2031/02/01, CUSIP 46515DSQ6: issue date 15 Feb 2026, principal 9,900,000.00, maturity 01 Feb 2031, interest rate 4.93%."
        >
          Israel Bonds purchase confirmation for ATRS, issue date February 15, 2026. Produced in ATRS’s September 11, 2026
          public-records response. A literal crop. The black boxes cover a name or mailing line, bank-account digits and the
          report number, which we removed, and the fiscal-agent account number, which the agency redacted. Select the image to
          open it at full size. <a href="/evidence/#SRC-ATR-004">Source notes</a>.
        </RecordFigure>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <details class="disclosure">
          <summary>See the funding and purchase records <Icon name="plus" /></summary>
          <div class="panel">
            <p>
              ATRS public-records response, September 11, 2026: the State Street activity report for the January 2 transfer,
              the Israel Bonds purchase confirmation, the manager’s July 31 holdings worksheet, and its second-quarter report.
              The linked images are literal crops.
            </p>
            <div class="stack">
              <a class="go" href="/media/atrs-funding-completed-2026-01-02.png"
                >Open the $50 million transfer excerpt <Icon name="external" /></a
              >
              <a class="go" href="/media/atrs-israel-bond-2026-02-15.png"
                >Open the $9.9 million purchase excerpt <Icon name="external" /></a
              >
            </div>
            <p class="provenance">
              Correction, September 28, 2026: we previously dated this funding to December 2025, from a December 29, 2025
              update to trustees that listed it among December payments. The transfer records show it was completed January
              2, 2026. <a href="/media/atrs-december-funding.png">The December excerpt remains available</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
              Source notes: <a href="/evidence/#SRC-ATR-003">transfer</a> · <a href="/evidence/#SRC-ATR-004">purchase</a> · <a
                href="/evidence/#SRC-ATR-005">holdings</a
              > · <a href="/evidence/#SRC-ATR-006">manager’s report</a> · <a href="/investments/#atrs">View the dated ledger</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="margin-grid chapter demand-note" aria-labelledby="demand-title">
      <h2 id="demand-title">End the support.<br /><em>Bring the money home.</em></h2>
      <p class="dateline">The demand</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>No new Israel Bonds. No renewals. No reinvestment. Organize now to end the financial relationship and keep the money out.</p>
        <a class="go" href="/how-we-win/">How we win <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <CloseStrip />
</Base>

<style>
  .onpage {
    margin-top: var(--s-6) !important;
    padding-top: var(--s-4);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--olive-screen);
  }
  .onpage__label {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--olive-screen);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  .onpage ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-2) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0 var(--s-5);
  }
  .onpage a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 2.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .issue {
    padding-block: var(--s-6) var(--s-8);
  }
  .chapter,
  .institution {
    padding-block: var(--s-8) 0;
  }
  .chapter > h2,
  .institution > h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
  }
  .margin-grid > .prose {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
  }
  .institution {
    margin-top: var(--s-7);
    border-top: 0.75rem solid var(--olive);
    padding-top: var(--s-6);
  }
  .institution > h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
  }
  .institution > .provenance {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .wide {
    max-width: 62rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .margin-grid > .wide {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
  .statement {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.05;
    color: var(--red-deep);
    margin-block: var(--s-5) !important;
  }
  .call-quote {
    margin: var(--s-5) 0;
    padding: var(--s-4) 0;
    border-block: 2px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.12;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .demands {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0 !important;
  }
  .demands li {
    padding: var(--s-3) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .demands li + li {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
  }

  /* How the ATRS money moved: four dated states in order, never summed. */
  .steps {
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
  }
  .steps__title {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }
  .steps ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-4) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    border-top: 3px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .steps li {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15em;
    padding: var(--s-4) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .steps__date {
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .steps__state {
    color: var(--olive-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .steps__amt {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .steps__bar {
    display: block;
    height: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: var(--s-2);
    background: var(--screen), var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .steps__bar span {
    display: block;
    width: 19.71%;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--olive);
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .steps ol {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
    .steps li {
      padding-right: var(--s-4);
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
    .steps li + li {
      padding-left: var(--s-4);
      border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    }
  }
  .steps__foot {
    margin-top: var(--s-3);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    max-width: 44em;
  }
  .demand-note {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule);
    margin-top: var(--s-8);
  }
</style>
